WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.942
Your donor database knows who gave.

2
00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:04.178
It cannot tell you who is about to stop.

3
00:00:04.274 --> 00:00:07.747
Donor Signals scores every donor you have,
overnight.

4
00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:09.942
It tells you who to call first.

5
00:00:09.952 --> 00:00:11.881
It starts where your board starts.

6
00:00:11.891 --> 00:00:15.828
Did last year's donors come back, is the
money ahead or behind, and did the new

7
00:00:15.838 --> 00:00:17.088
ones stay.

8
00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:20.611
Each one carries what it should be
measured against, so you can tell a good

9
00:00:20.621 --> 00:00:22.795
year from a lucky one.

10
00:00:22.805 --> 00:00:26.220
Under that, your whole file sorted by what
each donor needs next.

11
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.552
With the money at stake on every group, so
you can see where an hour of your week is

12
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.561
worth the most.

13
00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:33.802
At risk is where most tools get it wrong.

14
00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:36.890
They pick a number of days and call
everyone past it lapsed.

15
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.909
Storyraise measures each donor against
their own rhythm.

16
00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:42.950
A monthly donor who has missed three gifts
is late.

17
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.091
An annual donor who gave in November is
not.

18
00:00:46.188 --> 00:00:49.522
And the list leads with the most at stake.

19
00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:54.247
Every donor comes with the reason they are
in front of you, in a sentence, not a

20
00:00:54.257 --> 00:00:55.803
score you have to decode.

21
00:00:55.813 --> 00:00:59.309
And because Storyraise publishes your
reports, it knows something your database

22
00:00:59.319 --> 00:01:00.366
does not:

23
00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:02.816
who is still reading you after they
stopped giving.

24
00:01:02.826 --> 00:01:05.626
Those are the ones you can win back.

25
00:01:05.636 --> 00:01:08.094
Then the part that makes this worth your
morning.

26
00:01:08.794 --> 00:01:11.872
Every group comes with two things you can
make for them, already written for that

27
00:01:11.882 --> 00:01:12.865
moment.

28
00:01:12.962 --> 00:01:18.118
Record one video for everyone who just
gave, or build a report that shows them

29
00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:19.965
what their money did.

30
00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:21.939
Open one and half the work is done.

31
00:01:21.949 --> 00:01:25.747
Start from a draft written for this group,
a template, or a report you have already

32
00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:26.571
published.

33
00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:31.285
The group comes with you, and each donor
gets a page with their own name on it.

34
00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:33.555
And once a month, the longer view.

35
00:01:33.652 --> 00:01:36.254
Retention by gift size is the one that
changes plans.

36
00:01:36.264 --> 00:01:39.110
Your smallest donors come back about a
third of the time.

37
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.582
Donors giving in the thousands come back
nearly nine times in ten.

38
00:01:42.592 --> 00:01:45.392
That is where next year's plan comes from.

39
00:01:45.402 --> 00:01:48.550
All of this comes out of the donor data
you already have.

40
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.086
Give it one morning, and find out who has
been waiting to hear from you.
